[{"id":"29671","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">What was the reign of Qutubuddin Aybak?</span></p>","option_1":"1188-1199","option_2":"1199-1205","option_3":"1206-1210","option_4":"1210-1216","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 1206-1210.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Qutubuddin Aybak, a former slave (Mamluk) of Muhammad Ghori, founded the Delhi Sultanate's Mamluk (Slave) Dynasty and ruled from 1206 to 1210 CE. He is credited with laying the foundation of the Qutub Minar and constructing the Quwwat-ul-Islam Mosque in Delhi. His reign ended with his accidental death in 1210 while playing chaugan (polo) in Lahore.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29672","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The Nizam State Railway was taken over by the centre in which of the following years?</span></p>","option_1":"1952","option_2":"1950","option_3":"1956","option_4":"1954","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 1950.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Nizam State Railway, which served the erstwhile princely state of Hyderabad, was taken over by the Government of India (Central Government) in 1950, following the integration of the princely states after independence. It was subsequently merged into the Indian Railways system and later became part of the Central Railway/South Central Railway zones.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29673","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In which region was the Khokhar tribe very influential during the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries?</span></p>","option_1":"Assam","option_2":"Gujarat","option_3":"Bengal","option_4":"Punjab","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Punjab.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Khokhar tribe was a powerful and influential Jat/Rajput clan based in the Punjab region (particularly around the Salt Range and western Punjab) during the 13th and 14th centuries. They frequently resisted and rebelled against the Delhi Sultanate's authority, most notably during the reigns of Balban and later Sultans.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29674","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In ______, the Life Insurance sector was nationalised and the Life Insurance Corporation came into existence in the same year.</span></p>","option_1":"1961","option_2":"1949","option_3":"1956","option_4":"1951","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 1956.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The life insurance business in India was nationalised in 1956 through the Life Insurance Corporation Act, 1956, by merging over 245 private insurance companies and provident societies. The Life Insurance Corporation of India (LIC) was established in the same year as a result of this nationalisation.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29675","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following rivers is NOT the tributary of Ganga?</span></p>","option_1":"Ghaghara","option_2":"Kosi","option_3":"Mahadayi","option_4":"Gandak","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Mahadayi.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Ghaghara, Kosi, and Gandak are all major left-bank tributaries of the river Ganga, originating from the Himalayas and joining the Ganga in the Bihar plains. The Mahadayi (also known as the Mandovi in Goa) is an independent west-flowing river originating in the Western Ghats (Karnataka) and draining into the Arabian Sea, having no connection with the Ganga river system.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29676","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Tropic of cancer passes through which of the following states of India?</span></p>","option_1":"Maharashtra","option_2":"Mizoram","option_3":"Andhra Pradesh","option_4":"Goa","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Mizoram.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Tropic of Cancer (23°30' N) passes through eight Indian states: Gujarat, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Tripura, and Mizoram. Among the given options, only Mizoram lies on this latitude; Maharashtra, Andhra Pradesh, and Goa lie south of the Tropic of Cancer.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29677","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">What is the name of the food pipe which takes food from the mouth to the stomach in humans?</span></p>","option_1":"Bladder","option_2":"Oesophagus","option_3":"Intestine","option_4":"Spleen","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Oesophagus.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The oesophagus (food pipe/gullet) is a muscular tube that connects the pharynx (throat) to the stomach, transporting food via peristaltic movements. The bladder stores urine, the intestine is involved in digestion/absorption after the stomach, and the spleen is part of the lymphatic system, filtering blood — none of these transport food from mouth to stomach.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29678","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In which modern-day region of India was the 'Panchala' Mahajanapada located?</span></p>","option_1":"Punjab","option_2":"Andhra Pradesh","option_3":"Eastern Uttar Pradesh","option_4":"Western Uttar Pradesh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Western Uttar Pradesh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Panchala was one of the sixteen Mahajanapadas of ancient India, located in the region corresponding to present-day western Uttar Pradesh (roughly between the Himalayas and the river Chambal, with the Ganga dividing it into North and South Panchala). Its capitals were Ahichhatra (North Panchala) and Kampilya (South Panchala).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29679","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">As per provisions of the Inter-State River Water Disputes (ISRWD) Act, 1956, the Tribunal shall consist of a Chairman and two other Members nominated by the ______.</span></p>","option_1":"President of India","option_2":"Chief Minister of concerned States","option_3":"Central Government after consultation with State Governments","option_4":"Chief Justice of India","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Chief Justice of India.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per the Inter-State River Water Disputes (ISRWD) Act, 1956, when the Central Government constitutes a Water Disputes Tribunal, its Chairman and two other Members are nominated by the Chief Justice of India from among sitting judges of the Supreme Court or High Courts. This ensures judicial independence in adjudicating inter-state river water disputes.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29680","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">'Titodo' is a traditional folk dance performed by unmarried people in which Indian state?</span></p>","option_1":"Gujarat","option_2":"Karnataka","option_3":"Meghalaya","option_4":"West Bengal","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Gujarat.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Titodo' (also spelt Tittoda) is a traditional folk dance of Gujarat, performed by unmarried young men and women, typically during festive and social occasions, forming part of the state's rich tradition of folk dances alongside Garba and Dandiya Raas.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29681","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The 'Saptak Music Festival' in Gujarat is celebrated in which city?</span></p>","option_1":"Surat","option_2":"Rajkot","option_3":"Vadodara","option_4":"Ahmedabad","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Ahmedabad.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Saptak Annual Music Festival, one of India's prominent classical music festivals featuring both Hindustani and Carnatic musicians, is organised annually in Ahmedabad, Gujarat, by the Saptak School of Music.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29682","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following approach of human development is associated with Amartya Sen?</span></p>","option_1":"Capability Approach","option_2":"Basic Needs Approach","option_3":"Welfare Approach","option_4":"Income Approach","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Capability Approach.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Capability Approach to human development was pioneered by economist and Nobel laureate Amartya Sen, who argued that development should be measured by the real freedoms and capabilities people have to lead the lives they value, rather than merely by income or welfare. This approach laid the conceptual foundation for the UNDP's Human Development Index.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29683","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The North-Eastern Space Applications Centre is in which of the following state?</span></p>","option_1":"Meghalaya","option_2":"Assam","option_3":"Nagaland","option_4":"Arunachal Pradesh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Meghalaya.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The North Eastern Space Applications Centre (NESAC), a joint venture of the Department of Space (ISRO) and the North Eastern Council, is located in Umiam, near Shillong, Meghalaya. It works on applying space technology for the socio-economic development of the North-Eastern region.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29684","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">As per Census 2011, which of the following states experienced a decline in rural population compared to 2001?</span></p>","option_1":"Kerala","option_2":"Gujarat","option_3":"Karnataka","option_4":"Andhra Pradesh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Kerala.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per Census 2011 data, Kerala was the only major state to record an absolute decline in its rural population compared to Census 2001, largely attributed to rapid urbanisation, migration to urban/semi-urban areas, and reclassification of many rural settlements as urban/census towns.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29685","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In May 2025, which country launched the first AI satellites to build the world's first supercomputer in orbit?</span></p>","option_1":"India","option_2":"China","option_3":"USA","option_4":"South Korea","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - China.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In May 2025, China launched the first batch of its planned constellation of AI-powered satellites, aimed at building the world's first space-based supercomputer network capable of processing data directly in orbit, reducing dependence on ground-based data centres for large-scale AI computation.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29686","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">What of the following is an example of object moving under uniform circular motion?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Motion of the Moon</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Motion of the Earth</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Uniform circular motion refers to movement along a circular (or near-circular) path at a constant speed. The Moon's revolution around the Earth and the Earth's revolution around the Sun both approximate uniform circular motion (their orbits are actually slightly elliptical, but at the level tested in SSC exams, both are treated as examples of bodies moving in roughly circular paths at near-constant speed), making both I and II correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29687","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">What is the total length (in km) of National Waterway-2?</span></p>","option_1":"451","option_2":"891","option_3":"1108","option_4":"1720","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 891.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>National Waterway-2 (NW-2) is the Brahmaputra river route from Sadiya to Dhubri in Assam, with a total length of approximately 891 km, making it one of the important inland waterways declared under the National Waterways Act, 2016.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29688","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The 7th Asia-Pacific Regional Committee of the International Solar Alliance was held at ______.</span></p>","option_1":"Bangkok","option_2":"New Delhi","option_3":"Colombo","option_4":"Male","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Colombo.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The 7th Asia-Pacific Regional Committee (RC) meeting of the International Solar Alliance (ISA) — a treaty-based intergovernmental organisation co-founded by India to promote solar energy — was hosted in Colombo, Sri Lanka, bringing together member countries from the Asia-Pacific region to discuss solar energy cooperation and initiatives.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29689","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The National Green Hydrogen Mission in India aims to achieve a targeted production capacity of _____ million tonnes per annum green hydrogen by 2030.</span></p>","option_1":"2","option_2":"3","option_3":"5","option_4":"7","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 5.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The National Green Hydrogen Mission, approved by the Government of India, targets a green hydrogen production capacity of at least 5 million tonnes per annum by 2030, along with associated renewable energy capacity addition of about 125 GW, aimed at making India a global hub for green hydrogen production and export.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29690","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Who is the author of the book 'India Divided'?</span></p>","option_1":"Jawaharlal Nehru","option_2":"Rajendra Prasad","option_3":"B.R. Ambedkar","option_4":"Vallabhbhai Patel","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Rajendra Prasad.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'India Divided' is a book authored by Dr. Rajendra Prasad, who later became the first President of India. Published in 1946, the book presents Prasad's views opposing the partition of India and analyses the historical and political factors behind the demand for Pakistan.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29691","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In Gymnastics, women have ______ apparatus used during competitions.</span></p>","option_1":"2","option_2":"4","option_3":"6","option_4":"8","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 4.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In women's artistic gymnastics, competitors perform on four apparatus: Vault, Uneven Bars, Balance Beam, and Floor Exercise. This differs from men's artistic gymnastics, which involves six apparatus (Floor, Pommel Horse, Rings, Vault, Parallel Bars, and Horizontal Bar).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29692","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The image represents one of the signs given by umpire in the game of cricket. What does it call? (Umpire shown with both arms crossed over the chest)</span></p>","option_1":"Decision change","option_2":"Four","option_3":"Dead ball","option_4":"Short run","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Decision change.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In cricket umpiring signals, crossing both arms across the chest indicates that the umpire is changing/overturning a decision previously given. A boundary 'Four' is signalled by waving one arm from side to side, 'Dead ball' is signalled by crossing and uncrossing the arms below the waist, and 'Short run' is signalled by tapping the shoulder with the hand — none of which match arms crossed over the chest.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29693","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which Mughal emperor began the architectural development of Fatehpur Sikri?</span></p>","option_1":"Babur","option_2":"Akbar","option_3":"Jahangir","option_4":"Shah Jahan","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Akbar.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Emperor Akbar began the architectural development of Fatehpur Sikri in 1571, building it as his capital city near Agra to commemorate Sufi saint Salim Chishti, who had blessed him with a son (Jahangir). The city, however, was abandoned within a couple of decades, largely due to water scarcity.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29694","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In Haripala's \"Sangeeta Sudhakara\", written in the _____ century A.D., the terms Carnatic and Hindustani are found for the first time.</span></p>","option_1":"6","option_2":"14","option_3":"18","option_4":"8","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 14.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Sangeeta Sudhakara', a musical treatise authored by Haripala (Haripaladeva) in the 14th century A.D., is historically significant as the earliest known text in which the terms 'Carnatic' and 'Hindustani' appear, marking one of the earliest documented distinctions between South Indian and North Indian classical music traditions.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29695","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"772","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Who was the first Indian astronaut to travel into space?</span></p>","option_1":"Rakesh Sharma","option_2":"Raja Chari","option_3":"Sirisha Bandla","option_4":"Ravish Malhotra","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Rakesh Sharma.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Squadron Leader Rakesh Sharma became the first Indian citizen to travel into space, flying aboard the Soviet Soyuz T-11 mission in April 1984 as part of the Indo-Soviet joint space programme, spending about eight days aboard the Salyut 7 space station. Ravish Malhotra was his backup for the mission but did not fly; Raja Chari and Sirisha Bandla are Indian-origin American astronauts, not Indian citizens.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]